e for the  defendant  um so one of the reasons for writing the  book was  i guess to find a space for people like  me  to feel like actually we do belong and  so  that kind of experience i had with queer  as folk where i saw myself  the hope is that the book will do that  for younger people  i i know from from a couple of friends  just how significant  the russell t davis drama was to to them  and and  not just in identifying their own  sexuality but in the process of them  them coming out i wonder how that is  dealt with in the book  given that you grew up in such a devout  religious family  yeah i mean you know i i know the  headlines always talk about the fact  that um  my dad called a witch doctor to the  house but  you know that was people think of that  as a really nasty thing to have done  but it was an act of love perhaps a  misguided act but an act of love and  ultimately  you know my dad is one of my and my mom  are two of my biggest advocates today  and that's something that i'm so  grateful for  and that was the other reason to write  the book was to say that  there is more than one narrative it  doesn't just have to be about  oppression of minorities well they must  certainly have been instrumental in your  your push your your love of education  the drive that took you all the way from  east london  to an oxford college to study law  yeah i mean i'm not sure i would call it  a love of education i don't know how  many people  love education um but yeah i mean  completely you know they they were  immigrants themselves and  they we talk often about um  immigrants unfortunately usually it's in  a negative tone but they have  a really hard work ethic and they knew  that the key the only way that that  their children were going to make out of  the circumstance of you know being in a  council house  was education and so i remember like  when we were kids if we wanted to go and  go to the park my mum and dad would say  you can go to the park but first  you have to do a comprehension exercise  or go do your homework or  read something and i resented it at the  time and unfortunately i think so much  as with so much of life you realize as  you get older how right your parents  were  so it's thanks a lot of this is thanks  to them well i wonder then how you might  have felt if you had been doing ua  levels in  in 2020 i don't know whether you had an  unconditional or not from oxford whether  you needed your grades  but if you needed grades to be booted  out of school for several months and  then ultimately to find your results  downgraded because of the  the school that you went to i wonder  what your view might have been at that  point  you know only it's something like only  2.2 percent of  uh kids from the least uh from from  sorry from the most deprived backgrounds  end up with the a-level grades to go to  the top  universities um you know i'm appalled by  the way  that young people to today have been  treated  uh and unfortunately it has  disproportionately  affected people from more deprived  backgrounds i mean  anecdotally and i'm not sure if this is  true or not but i've heard that eaton  which i do now know  um didn't have any of their grades uh  downgraded and i this is not about  eaten it's just about the fact of um  fairness  and you've got young people who have  worked so hard who do  come from really difficult backgrounds  who have done everything they possibly  can  who are now being told well sorry  because of some formula we've decided  that you're a star  which you would have got yeah um we  don't think he would have got mohsen i'm  i'm so sorry  we do have to leave it there but it is  an inspirational uh story  entitled a dutiful boy as i said just  carefully place  over your shoulder thank you for joining  us
 

